For each fixed integer $k\ge3$ there are constants $C_k,c_k,\varepsilon_k>0$ such that, for every $N\ge2$, $$\label{ap:density-bound}
 r_k(N)\le C_kN\exp\bigl(-c_k(\log N)^{\varepsilon_k}\bigr).$$ Equivalently, there is $A_k\ge1$ such that an $\alpha$-dense subset of $[N]$ contains a nonconstant $k$-term progression whenever $$\label{ap:threshold}
 \log N\ge A_k\bigl(2+\log(1/\alpha)\bigr)^{A_k},
 \qquad 0<\alpha\le1.$$

There is $C'_k>0$ such that every $k$-term-progression-free set $A\subseteq\mathbb N$ satisfies, for all sufficiently large real $x$, $$\sum_{\substack{a\in A\\a>x}}\frac1a
 \le C'_k(\log x)^{1-\varepsilon_k}
                e^{-c_k(\log x)^{\varepsilon_k}}.$$ In particular, for every $0<a<c_k$, this tail is at most $C_{k,a}e^{-a(\log x)^{\varepsilon_k}}$.
